  <section><h2>Reflections & Lessons Learned</h2>
    <h3>Understanding the Learning Curve</h3>
    <p>I initially aimed to make search useful to both experienced users and people unfamiliar with federal spending. Testing with novices showed how difficult that balance would be. When asked how much was spent on construction in Virginia, users often entered “construction” and “Virginia” into keyword search. That was valid, but location filters and relevant industry or product/service codes would define the needed records more precisely.</p>
    <p>The search could not behave like a general-purpose search engine and interpret an entire plain-language question. I leaned toward preserving the depth experienced users relied on while helping novices learn through tooltips, category browsing, and explanatory resources.</p>

    <h3>Designing Within Technical and Data Constraints</h3>
    <p>The experience had to account for limitations in query interpretation, record indexing, and download preparation. Structured filters, category browsing, and contextual guidance helped users define searches. When downloads took time to prepare, I designed a separate status tab that explained the wait and provided the file when ready.</p>
    <p>Indexing also affected discoverability. Main search covered contract records but not underlying transaction descriptions, so a contract described as “office supplies” could contain a transaction for “pencils” yet remain absent from a keyword search for pencils. A separate transaction search gave users a way to investigate at that more detailed level.</p>

    <h3>Staying Flexible Through Collaboration</h3>
    <p>Scaling the initial release back to a table view reinforced the importance of separating the longer-term design from what could be delivered in a particular release. Discussing limitations with engineering and the client helped establish a workable path forward while keeping the intended user benefits in view.</p>
  </section>

  <section><h2>What I’d Refine Today</h2>
    <div class="refine-grid">
      <div><h3>Pre-Made Searches</h3><p>Useful starting points for common topics such as defense spending and education, demonstrating how filters work together.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Search Tutorials</h3><p>Practical examples showing how to choose and combine filters and understand the scope of results.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Condensed Summary</h3><p>Bring key totals into a concise overview before users explore individual records and deeper analytical views.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Time Comparisons</h3><p>Compare values across periods to reveal trends, shifts in priorities, and changes in funding.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Compact Analysis</h3><p>Bring more related visualizations into one cohesive overview with less scrolling.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

